<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The sandbox, and the one grant it needs.

  One file for three bundles, because nothing in it is per-application: each
  opens the file a person chose and writes nothing at all.

  **The entitlement does nothing until it is inside a signature.** An unsigned
  bundle is not sandboxed, so every measurement of sandbox behaviour has to be
  made against a signed one, and an Apple Development certificate is enough for
  that. This is why `build-app.sh` signs even for a development build, and why
  nothing about the sandbox can be settled from Linux.

  `files.user-selected.read-only`, because these applications read and do not
  write. A capability asked for and unused is a question at review with no good
  answer, and a viewer asking permission to write is exactly that question.

  The editing branch will widen this to `read-write`, which is one word in one
  file. An earlier draft of this comment argued for asking now on the grounds
  that widening later would move the sandbox container a person's grants live
  in. That was wrong: the container is keyed by the bundle identifier and an
  entitlement change does not move it. The wrong reason is recorded here rather
  than quietly deleted, because it is the kind of claim that would otherwise be
  repeated.

  Nothing else. No network, no broad filesystem access, no device, and no
  keychain group even though the provisioning profile grants one.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
